# PROVENANCE — kernel-side payload

## 4.9.84/kmod-0607-glibc-9.1.0/

Prebuilt vendor MI kernel modules, from the same build as the userspace
libraries in johnchia/sigmastar-lib:

    release_0607   built 2022-06-07 09:51-09:52
    project_commit ceb04c4
    sdk_commit     dc99390
    toolchain      GCC 9.1.0, glibc
    vermagic       4.9.84 SMP preempt mod_unload ARMv7 thumb2 p2v8

**The flavour is in the directory name because vermagic will not catch a
mismatch.** The vendor ships these under <libc>/<gcc> trees that are not one
source built several ways: across release_0601's own trees, mi_sys.ko differs by
27-29 imported kernel symbols, differs in `depends=` (mi_common under
glibc/9.1.0, empty under the others), and the module sets differ -- mi_ai.ko,
mi_ao.ko and gc4653_MIPI.ko exist only under glibc/9.1.0. vermagic is byte
identical across all of them and CONFIG_MODVERSIONS is off, so insmod accepts a
foreign module and it fails later at symbol resolution, or misbehaves.

Do not mix these with modules from another flavour directory.

Previously johnchia/sigmastar-sdk @ cdfe9e3e8421dc6929d1e55dce22ab54e7556fd4, under infinity6e/kmod/.

## 4.9.84/sensor-src/

From openipc/sensors @ 8143e4da181d39d569572519595ce4b7e1a9a02d, sigmastar half only; the hisilicon half
(916K) is not carried. Source, compiled here against the pinned kernel.

Only the families a thingino target can select are here -- infinity6e and
infinity6b0, the two SOC_FAMILY values soc/sigmastar/*.mk defines, both of which
probe to 4.9.84 and so belong under this level.

Upstream also ships infinity6 and infinity6c. Both were carried until 2026-08-10
and neither was reachable: sigmastar-sdk.mk builds sensor-src/$(SOC_FAMILY) and
nothing sets either value. infinity6c was additionally misfiled -- Infinity6C is
a 5.10.61 family, so its drivers sat under a directory level that asserts 4.9.84.

They arrived by mirroring upstream's sigmastar/ level wholesale. Upstream is not
keyed by kernel release at all, so the mirror had nothing to check the families
against; this level is our own and the copy imported past it. A family belongs
here only if its kernel probes to 4.9.84.

Restore either from the upstream hash above if a target appears -- infinity6c
symlinks two of its drivers across to infinity6e, so it must come back with that
link intact, and under a level matching its own kernel.

Makefile and sensor_config.c stay at this level because every family symlinks to
them, and the Makefile is upstream's verbatim -- including an infinity6c branch
that is now dead. Left alone so the file still diffs cleanly against upstream.

These are **not** vendor dumps. The vendor SDK has 68 drivers; OpenIPC ships 11
per family, renamed and reformatted, and the overlap has diverged -- gc4653, the
one this board uses, is retuned from 25fps to 30fps (Preview_MAX_FPS 25->30,
vts_25fps->vts_30fps, Preview_line_period 26667->22222, ENABLE_OTP_DD dropped,
plus register init table edits). Five of the eleven do not exist in the vendor
SDK at all. Re-deriving from the vendor tree would lose sensors and regress the
board to 25fps.

No flavour key, deliberately: drv_sensor.h is byte-identical between vendor
releases 0601 and 0902, and the built driver's only coupling to the vendor
bundle is four symbols -- CamOsMemAlloc, CamOsMemRelease, CamOsPrintf,
CamOsUsSleep -- all exported by mhal.ko. Kernel-coupled, not release-coupled.

Per family because the same filename is a different driver in each. Between the
two families here, sensor_imx335_mipi.c differs by 1434 lines, sensor_imx307_mipi.c
by 777 and sensor_gc2053_mipi.c by 10; drv_ms_cus_sensor.h differs too. Only
sensor_gc4653_mipi.c is identical. drv_sensor.h happens to match between these
two, so it is not what the split rests on -- the drivers are.

The family also selects which drivers exist at all: 11 for infinity6e, 18 for
infinity6b0.

## sensor-iq/<family>/

Per-sensor API bins -- IQ Tool "save Bin" output, loaded by the application via
MI_ISP_API_CmdLoadBinFile with user_key 1234. Distinct in both format and
purpose from iqfile/ below; the two are not interchangeable. Naming follows
ingenic-sdk/sensor-iq/<family>/.

## iqfile/

The CUS3A iqfile and its schema. Read from disk by CUS3A itself at AE init, from
whatever MI_ISP_GetIspRoot reports. Vendor location: project/board/i6e/iqfile/.

## venc_fw/<family>/

chagall.bin is **VENC firmware**, not ISP and not sensor, despite having lived
under sensor/firmware/ previously. Vendor location: project/board/i6e/venc_fw/,
authoritative copy sdk/mhal/chip_common/venc/hal/i6e/chagall.bin. Per chip --
the vendor ships a separate i6b0 copy.

